    {
      "page_title": "AOF Rewrite & Multi-Part AOF",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Log Grows Forever",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`INCR counter` a million times = a million entries",
            "...describing one integer of actual state",
            "Rewrite emits the shortest sequence that rebuilds it",
            "History discarded, current dataset kept"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "before:  INCR c   x 1,000,000",
            "after:   SET c 1000000",
            "# same dataset, one line instead of a million"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When It Fires"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "conf",
          "lines": [
            "auto-aof-rewrite-percentage 100  # grown 2x aof_base_size",
            "auto-aof-rewrite-min-size 64mb   # and past the floor",
            "# 0 disables auto rewrite entirely",
            "# then the log grows until the disk fills"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`BGREWRITEAOF` forks a child that walks memory",
            "The child writes a fresh, minimal base",
            "`aof_rewrite_scheduled:1` = waiting on a running child"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Old Design vs Redis 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Pre-7",
                "lines": [
                  "Child writes one file",
                  "Parent buffers new writes",
                  "Buffer appended, then rename",
                  "Memory-hungry and fiddly"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Multi-part AOF",
                "lines": [
                  "Child writes base only",
                  "Parent appends to a new incr",
                  "Manifest lists the parts",
                  "No rewrite buffer at all"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Inside appendonlydir/"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "matrix",
          "spec": {
            "rows": [
              [
                "*.manifest",
                "*.base.rdb"
              ],
              [
                "*.1.incr.aof",
                "*.2.incr.aof"
              ]
            ],
            "highlight": [],
            "caption": "appendonlydir/"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Manifest is updated atomically at the very end",
            "Old base and incr files deleted only after that",
            "A crash mid-rewrite leaves the old manifest valid"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Hybrid Format"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`aof-use-rdb-preamble yes`, default since 4.0",
            "Base is an RDB dump, not RESP commands",
            "Tail stays per-command, so durability is unchanged",
            "Loads dramatically faster than a plain AOF"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Still A Fork",
          "text": "A rewrite pays the same COW memory and `latest_fork_usec` stall as a BGSAVE — and the two never run at once."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Your Redis just restarted — how much did you lose? The honest answer is a config line, not a feeling: RDB gives you a forked point-in-time snapshot and minutes of loss, AOF gives you a command log with an fsync dial, and hybrid gives you most of both. 10 pages on snapshots, copy-on-write cost, the everysec-is-really-2s footnote, the AOF-beats-RDB load trap that boots an empty dataset, and the production config to just copy.",
